model_answer_en:
Idea
Keep two stacks: in receives every enqueue; out serves every dequeue. When out is empty, pop all of in and push onto out: the oldest element ends up on top of out, so popping out gives FIFO order.

Trace: enqueue 10, 20, 30 → in = [10, 20, 30 (top)], out = []. dequeue: out is empty, so move all → out = [30, 20, 10 (top)]; pop gives 10. enqueue 40 → in = [40]. dequeue → 20, then 30 (still in out), then out is empty again, move 40 → 40.

Code (Java, compiled and run)
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class TwoStackQueue {
    private final Deque<Integer> in = new ArrayDeque<>();   // stack for enqueue
    private final Deque<Integer> out = new ArrayDeque<>();  // stack for dequeue

    public void enqueue(int x) {
        in.push(x);                       // O(1)
    }

    public int dequeue() {
        if (out.isEmpty()) {              // refill only when out is empty
            while (!in.isEmpty()) {
                out.push(in.pop());       // reverses the order: oldest on top
            }
        }
        if (out.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Queue underflow");
        }
        return out.pop();
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return in.isEmpty() && out.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TwoStackQueue q = new TwoStackQueue();
        q.enqueue(10);
        q.enqueue(20);
        q.enqueue(30);
        System.out.println(q.dequeue());  // 10
        q.enqueue(40);
        System.out.println(q.dequeue());  // 20
        System.out.println(q.dequeue());  // 30
        System.out.println(q.dequeue());  // 40
        System.out.println(q.isEmpty());  // true
    }
}

Output
10
20
30
40
true

Complexity
| Operation | Worst case | Amortised |
|---|---|---|
| enqueue | O(1) | O(1) |
| dequeue | O(n) (when out must be refilled) | O(1) |
Each element is pushed and popped at most twice in its life (once on each stack), so n operations cost O(n) in total. Space: O(n).

Can one stack do it? Not with stack operations alone; with recursion the call stack acts as the second stack.

