model_answer_en:
This is the activity-selection problem, solved by the greedy rule "earliest finish time first".

Algorithm:
Step 1: sort the requests by end time.
Step 2: lastEnd ← −∞; for each request [s, e] in that order, if s ≥ lastEnd, serve it and set lastEnd ← e.
Step 3: the served requests are a maximum set.

Language: Java

```java
import java.util.*;

public class MaxCustomers {
    // Returns the slots chosen by the earliest-finish-time greedy rule.
    static List<int[]> schedule(int[][] req) {
        int[][] r = req.clone();
        Arrays.sort(r, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[1], b[1])); // sort by end time
        List<int[]> chosen = new ArrayList<>();
        int lastEnd = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        for (int[] s : r) {
            if (s[0] >= lastEnd) {          // starts after the last chosen slot ends
                chosen.add(s);
                lastEnd = s[1];
            }
        }
        return chosen;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] req = {{9, 12}, {10, 11}, {11, 13}, {12, 14}, {13, 16}, {14, 15}, {15, 17}};
        List<int[]> c = schedule(req);
        System.out.println("Maximum customers: " + c.size());
        for (int[] s : c) System.out.println("[" + s[0] + ", " + s[1] + "]");
        int[][] trap = {{1, 10}, {2, 3}, {4, 5}};
        System.out.println("Trap case: " + schedule(trap).size());
    }
}
```

Output:
```
Maximum customers: 4
[10, 11]
[11, 13]
[14, 15]
[15, 17]
Trap case: 2
```

Trace: sorted by end: [10, 11], [9, 12], [11, 13], [12, 14], [14, 15], [13, 16], [15, 17]. Take [10, 11]; skip [9, 12] (9 < 11); take [11, 13]; skip [12, 14]; take [14, 15]; skip [13, 16]; take [15, 17]. Four customers are served.

Why sorting by start time is wrong: for [1, 10], [2, 3], [4, 5] it picks [1, 10] first and serves only 1 customer, while the earliest-finish rule serves 2 (the "Trap case" line).

Complexity: O(n log n) for the sort plus O(n) for the scan; O(n) extra space for the copy.

