model_answer_en:
Method (DFS-based): run DFS from every unvisited vertex in the order 0, 1, …, n − 1 and push a vertex onto a stack only after all its descendants are finished. Popping the stack gives a topological order, because every vertex is pushed after the vertices it points to.

Language: Java

```java
import java.util.*;

public class TopoSort {
    static List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
    static boolean[] visited;
    static Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

    static void dfs(int u) {
        visited[u] = true;
        for (int v : adj.get(u))
            if (!visited[v]) dfs(v);
        stack.push(u);              // push u after all its descendants
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 6;
        int[][] edges = {{5, 2}, {5, 0}, {4, 0}, {4, 1}, {2, 3}, {3, 1}};
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int[] e : edges) adj.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);
        visited = new boolean[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            if (!visited[i]) dfs(i);
        StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();
        while (!stack.isEmpty()) out.append(stack.pop()).append(' ');
        System.out.println(out.toString().trim());
    }
}
```

Output:
```
5 4 2 3 1 0
```

Trace (adjacency lists in input order: 5: 2, 0 | 4: 0, 1 | 2: 3 | 3: 1):
| DFS call | Pushed (in order) |
|---|---|
| dfs(0) | 0 |
| dfs(1) | 1 |
| dfs(2) → dfs(3) (1 already visited) | 3, 2 |
| i = 3: 3 already visited, skip | – |
| dfs(4) (0, 1 visited) | 4 |
| dfs(5) (2, 0 visited) | 5 |
Stack bottom to top: 0, 1, 3, 2, 4, 5, so popping prints 5 4 2 3 1 0.

Note: a DAG can have several valid orders. Kahn's algorithm (repeatedly remove an in-degree-0 vertex, smallest first) gives 4 5 0 2 3 1, which is also correct; if Kahn's algorithm removes fewer than n vertices, the graph has a cycle.

Complexity: O(V + E) time and O(V) space.

